# Vibecoding Life Pack — Skill Authoring

Skills for Cursor, Claude Code, and Codex are kept in the `vibecoding-life-pack`
git repository so they travel across platforms and devices. The repository is
the source of truth; the per-agent `~/.<agent>/skills/` directories are
installation targets (symlinks back to the repo).

## Where to author a skill

- **Cross-platform skill** (works on all three agents): author it at
  `canonical/shared/skills/<id>/SKILL.md` inside the `vibecoding-life-pack` repo.
  `install.sh` fans it out to `~/.cursor/skills`, `~/.claude/skills`, and
  `~/.codex/skills` automatically.
- **Platform-only skill**: author it under
  `canonical/<platform>/skills/<id>/SKILL.md` (e.g. `canonical/claude-code/skills/<id>/`).
  It only lands on that one platform.

After creating or changing a skill in the repo, run `./install.sh` from the repo
root so the consumer-dir symlinks are (re)created.

## If you created a skill directly in a consumer dir

If you (or an agent tool) wrote a skill straight into `~/.cursor/skills/<id>/`,
`~/.claude/skills/<id>/`, or `~/.codex/skills/<id>/` as real files (not
symlinks), do not worry: the life pack's `vibe-save` (which runs on Cursor
session end and from `sync.sh`) calls `bin/vibe-absorb-skills`, which moves that
skill into `canonical/shared/skills/<id>/` and replaces the original with a
symlink. So it still becomes shared across all platforms on the next save.

To absorb immediately without waiting for a save, run:

```bash
bin/vibe-absorb-skills
```

## New device / after git pull

After pulling the repo on a device (or running `./sync.sh`), shared skills are
re-linked into all three consumer dirs automatically by `sync.sh`. If you
 cloned fresh, run `./install.sh` once.

## Skill file format

Use a single `SKILL.md` with YAML frontmatter (`name`, `description`) followed
by markdown. This format is consumed by Cursor, Claude Code, and Codex alike,
so a shared skill needs only one file unless it truly requires platform-specific
fields.
